diagnosis_text:
No outage found. Location C (192.168.200.0/24) is fully connected at both L2 and L3. All four hosts in Location C can reach their default gateway and other sites. Evidence:
- From pc11, default route points to 192.168.200.1 via eth0, and ping to the gateway (router4, 192.168.200.1) succeeds with 0% loss and ~0.7–0.8 ms RTT. pc12–pc14 also successfully ping 192.168.200.1 with 0% loss.
- Intra-site L2/L3 is healthy: pc11–pc14 all reach each other with 0% loss.
- Inter-site reachability is intact in both directions: pc11 and pc12–pc14 can reach a remote host (pc4 at 172.16.10.4) with 0% loss and ~1.5–2.3 ms RTT; conversely, pc4 and pc5 can ping into Location C (pc11/pc12) with 0% loss.
This demonstrates that routing through router4 and the uplinks toward other locations are operational, and Location C is not isolated.

Root cause: No network isolation or device fault is present; the reported “totally cut off” state is not reproducible and likely stems from a host-specific or application-layer issue (e.g., DNS resolution or local firewall), not the network.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Close the isolation ticket. Advise affected users to verify DNS settings and test by IP; if issues persist, investigate client OS/firewall or application services rather than the network. === END ===